The origin of the rare-earth textural component is not yet fully understood. However, recent studies suggest that it arises from the preferential growth of grains with this orientation, driven by a solute drag effect due to the segregation of rare-earth elements at GBs [ 36 ]. This explanation aligns with our experimental observations. The XWAZ4111 alloy lacks this textural component and shows no elemental segregation at GBs. This absence is attributed to the depletion of yttrium by AlYMg and AlYZn particles. In contrast, the component is present in the other two alloys examined. Notably, in the XWZ411 alloy, the segregation is further intensified by the presence of zinc, which may be associated with the shift of this textural component closer to the  0001 \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
